WEBVTT

NOTE On-screen visual text only; this approved video has no audio track.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.350
An embedding represents an input using learned numbers.

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:14.200
In this illustrative plane, two vectors with nearby directions have high cosine similarity.

3
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:21.867
Closeness depends on the model and task; it does not guarantee two statements are equivalent.

4
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:29.917
In Spanish, banco can mean a bench or a financial bank. Nearby words help disambiguate it.

5
00:00:29.917 --> 00:00:36.767
Attention combines other positions using context-dependent weights.

6
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:44.117
The displayed weights are illustrative, not a complete explanation of reasoning.

7
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:51.783
For an invented continuation, three tokens have probabilities of sixty, thirty and ten percent.

8
00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:58.633
Sampling can select different tokens from that same distribution.

9
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:05.983
The selected token enters the context and changes the next step.

10
00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:13.333
The prompt shows two examples of converting a name into a label.

11
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:20.183
The model applies the pattern to a third name during this call.

12
00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:27.533
Here the context changed, not the weights: this is not a fine-tuning session.

13
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:34.883
The question selects a relevant passage from the returns document.

14
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:41.733
That passage enters the context alongside the question.

15
00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:49.400
The answer must support its claim with the passage; retrieval does not prevent every error.

16
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:56.750
The model proposes checking the order status.

17
00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:03.600
The system validates permission before executing the tool.

18
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:10.950
The observation returns to the model, but every new step retains limits and controls.

